# personal-id-forbidden.regex
#
# Patterns that MUST NOT appear in any shipped framework artefact
# (cli/, skills/, agents/, commands/, templates/, scripts/, dev-tools/,
# AGENTS.md, README.md, docs/).
#
# Format: one extended-regex pattern per non-empty, non-comment line.
# Lines starting with `#` are comments. Each pattern is matched
# case-insensitively via perl -CSD (UTF-8-safe; BSD-compatible).
#
# Inline exclusion fence: lines between
#   <!-- gate:example-only -->
# and
#   <!-- /gate:example-only -->
# are exempt (counter-examples and teaching content).
#
# Consumers may extend this by pointing --regex at their own merged file.
# Do NOT add ecosystem-specific entries here — keep the framework contract
# neutral. Consumer extensions belong in the consumer's local copy.

# Operator personal handles / names (word-boundary, case-insensitive)
\boperator\b
\boperator\b
\boperator\b
\bmember\b

# Ecosystem-specific hostnames / project names.
# The host family is matched as a prefix: an earlier `\bhost-dev\b` entry
# could not see `host-devs` (no word boundary between "dev" and "s"), nor
# the -prod / -www / -db / -ai siblings, so every one of them shipped.
\bhost-[a-z0-9]+
\bdev-box\b

# Consumer project names. A consumer's project name has no place in a
# framework that any project can adopt. This one was already disclosed by the
# narrower Vault-path entry it replaces (`arcanada/client`), so listing the bare
# name adds nothing a reader could not already see. Further consumer names go
# in the private overlay (see below), never here.
\bclient\b

# Consumer task IDs. `DEV-` is a universal area prefix, and this repository
# uses it for synthetic fixtures in the 0xxx and 9xxx ranges only. A DEV-1xxx
# to DEV-8xxx number is a real task from a consumer project's tracker cited as
# provenance; describe the incident instead of citing its ticket.
\bDEV-[1-8][0-9]{3}\b

# Consumer-specific tracker fields. A field named after one vendor's tracker
# (e.g. an Asana GID) couples a public contract to one customer's tooling; use
# a generic tracker reference field instead.
\basana_?gid\b

# Absolute home-directory paths name a person and a machine. Use $HOME, `~`,
# or a placeholder such as /Users/example, /home/example, /Users/YOUR_USER,
# or /home/<user>; /home/app is the conventional container service account. Matched case-sensitively so a URL path such as
# /v2/users/login is not mistaken for a macOS home. /home/runner and /home/dependabot are GitHub-hosted runner
# accounts, not people.
(?-i:/Users/)(?!example\b|YOUR_USER\b)[A-Za-z0-9_][A-Za-z0-9._-]*
/home/(?!example\b|app\b|runner\b|dependabot\b|linuxbrew\b)[A-Za-z0-9_][A-Za-z0-9._-]*

# The same home directory as encoded in an agent runtime's per-project state
# directory name (e.g. ~/.claude/projects/-Users-<name>-<project>/).
(?-i:-(?:Users|home)-)(?!example-)[A-Za-z0-9._]+-

# A tailnet's MagicDNS suffix is unique to one organisation's network.
\btail[0-9a-f]{6}\.ts\.net\b

# Personal Asana PAT variable names (any ASANA_*_PAT pattern)
ASANA_[A-Z_]*PAT

# 16-digit numeric IDs (Asana workspace/user GIDs)
\b[0-9]{16}\b

# Real public IPv4 — handled by heuristic, NOT by a literal denylist.
#
# This file ships in a PUBLIC repository. A denylist of literal infrastructure
# addresses would publish exactly what this gate exists to suppress: the file
# becomes its own leak, and a reader gets a ready-made role-to-address map.
# Literal addresses therefore MUST NOT be added here.
#
# The floor is the heuristic in scripts/personal-id-gate.sh
# (sub is_real_public_ipv4): it flags ANY globally-routable public IPv4 in the
# shipped surface, so a new, never-listed address is caught with no edit to
# this file. Reserved and documentation ranges are excluded there (RFC 1918,
# RFC 5737 TEST-NET, loopback, link-local, 100.64/10 CGNAT, and 4th-octet-0
# network/version strings).
#
# CGNAT mesh addresses (100.64/10) are deliberately outside the heuristic —
# they are not globally routable, and flagging the whole range would break
# legitimate documentation. An operator who needs specific mesh addresses
# caught supplies them through the private overlay, which the gate merges
# additively when present:
#
#   ${DATARIM_LOCAL:-$HOME/.claude/local}/config/personal-id-forbidden.regex
#
# That path is gitignored and machine-local. Override for tests/CI with
# DATARIM_PERSONAL_ID_OVERLAY=<file>.

# ssh root@ to a literal IPv4 (entry-point leak — use ssh root@<host> placeholder instead)
ssh root@[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}
